[REJECTED.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2107.03097/REJECTED.md) documents the decision and cost comparison.

The paper’s Theorem 1 completely classifies the solutions, while Lemma 6 exposes constant witnesses such as `(1,0)` and `(0,1)` for every \(n\ge3\). Thus:

- Track A fails: a constant-time attack succeeds on every instance.
- Track B fails: the mechanical and compact routes are identical—zero search and at most six verification operations.
- The paper’s expensive PARI/GP, Baker–Davenport, and LLL computations establish that no additional solutions exist; they are unnecessary for producing one witness.

An eight-parameter audit confirmed the constant attack succeeds 8/8. Artificially hiding these witnesses with coordinate transformations would create an unrelated equivalence puzzle rather than paper-backed hardness. [Paper and full text](https://arxiv.org/html/2107.03097v1).
