The paper’s unmodified equation is unusable, but a paper-native Track B construction does survive the Step 0 test: transport the cubic by an integral determinant-one change of variables, then give a bounded lattice box containing exactly one transported theorem solution. Theorem 1 proves uniqueness in that box; the checker only substitutes the submitted pair.

I’m using the paper’s own Section 3–4 geometry of “solutions as rational approximants to roots” for the compact route: the hidden change crowds the three projective roots around one primitive rational direction. A full bounded scan is millions of exact updates, while Vieta’s root centroid plus continued-fraction reconstruction is short once that structure is noticed. I’ll report both algorithms explicitly as Track B, including the successful compact solver rather than concealing it.
